      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mount Union sprang up in the late 19th century as a small agricultural hub near Cotton Grove, thanks to the railroad’s 1850s expansion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mount_union_iowa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits close to the Skunk River, which shapes local drainage patterns across the prairie.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mount_union_iowa",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early settlers were drawn to Mount Union’s fertile prairie lands, making it a prime spot for 19th‑century agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mount_union_iowa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Main Street still showcases 19th‑century commercial buildings, including the Johnson Harness Shop and the historic Smith Drugstore and Ice Cream Parlor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mount_union_iowa",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mount Union once operated as an incorporated city with its own government, post office, schools, and businesses until residents chose unincorporation amid financial challenges.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
